{
  // What gets published, which is a strict subset of what gets type-checked.
  //
  // `tsconfig.json` covers `ops` and `harness` so the tests and the runner are
  // held to the same strictness as everything else. None of that ships: the
  // harness imports vitest and the Dawn binding, so publishing it would put two
  // devDependencies on a consumer's runtime import graph to deliver code that
  // only ever runs against this repo's own suite.
  //
  // The entry points are named rather than globbed by directory so the program
  // is exactly the reachable graph — an op's `index.ts` pulls in its
  // `reference.ts` and stops there. Test-only helpers beside them
  // (`ops/*/cases.ts`) are reached by no entry point and so are never emitted,
  // which is why there is no exclude list to keep in sync.
  "extends": "./tsconfig.json",
  "compilerOptions": {
    "noEmit": false,
    "rootDir": ".",
    "outDir": "dist",
    "declaration": true,
    // The comments in this repo carry the reasoning, and that reasoning is worth
    // reading — but it is worth reading in the source, not in a bundle a browser
    // downloads. `declaration` keeps the JSDoc, so hovering a symbol in an
    // editor still shows why the op behaves the way it does.
    "removeComments": true,
    // Op code depends on no ambient types at all. Stating that here rather than
    // inheriting `["@webgpu/types", "node"]` makes the build fail if a reference
    // ever reaches for a `GPUBuffer` or a `node:` global, which would quietly
    // narrow where the published package can run.
    "types": [],
    // `llm/tokenizer.ts` needs `TextEncoder`/`TextDecoder` for its UTF-8
    // byte-fallback handling — standard Web APIs available in every runtime
    // this package targets (browsers, Node, Deno, Workers), unlike
    // `@webgpu/types` or `node:` globals. TypeScript's bundled `"DOM"` lib is
    // the only place their *types* live short of pulling in `@types/node`,
    // which is exactly the ambient dependency the `types: []` above exists to
    // keep out; it adds no WebGPU types (those come only from the excluded
    // `@webgpu/types` package), so it does not weaken that guard.
    "lib": ["ES2022", "DOM"]
  },
  // `llm/` entries are named one at a time, unlike `ops/*/index.ts`, and the
  // difference is not stylistic. An op's directory *is* its API: adding an op
  // and widening the published surface are the same act, so a glob states the
  // intent correctly. `llm/` is not that — it holds ~50 files, and several of
  // them (`engine-q8-resident`, `browser-weights`, `real-model-weights`,
  // `idb-chunk-store`, `storage-quota`) are working code nobody has decided to
  // freeze as API yet. Globbing it would collapse "compile this" and "let
  // consumers import this" into one decision, and #106's wiring work would
  // widen the surface as a side effect. The three below are what a downstream
  // consumer asked for by name (issue #138); each is self-contained, importing
  // nothing and touching no ambient type, which is why they build under
  // `types: []`.
  "include": [
    "ops/*/index.ts",
    "llm/tokenizer.ts",
    "llm/tokenizer-bpe.ts",
    "llm/kv-cache.ts",
    "llm/reshape.ts",
    "llm/sampler.ts"
  ]
}
